WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.633
Terraform.

2
00:00:01.933 --> 00:00:05.063
Clicking around a cloud console leaves no record.

3
00:00:05.063 --> 00:00:08.767
Terraform turns that into code you can review and rebuild.

4
00:00:09.967 --> 00:00:13.626
Terraform is infrastructure as code, declaratively.

5
00:00:13.626 --> 00:00:17.500
You describe buckets, clusters and roles in HCL files.

6
00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:19.996
Terraform then makes reality match.

7
00:00:19.996 --> 00:00:24.200
Changes become reviewable, since the plan goes in the pull request.

8
00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:26.804
They are also repeatable and auditable.

9
00:00:26.804 --> 00:00:29.167
Think of it as git blame for your cloud.

10
00:00:30.367 --> 00:00:32.033
The workflow is the whole point.

11
00:00:32.333 --> 00:00:34.267
You write the desired state in code.

12
00:00:34.567 --> 00:00:42.033
Plan compares it to the state file, Terraform's record of what exists, and shows what would be created, changed or destroyed.

13
00:00:42.333 --> 00:00:44.467
Apply executes precisely that diff.

14
00:00:44.767 --> 00:00:46.467
And then updates the state file.

15
00:00:47.667 --> 00:00:49.433
Learn to read a plan like a diff.

16
00:00:49.733 --> 00:00:51.333
A plus creates a resource.

17
00:00:51.633 --> 00:00:53.333
A tilde changes it in place.

18
00:00:53.633 --> 00:00:56.525
Minus slash plus means destroy and recreate.

19
00:00:56.525 --> 00:00:58.300
That is where outages hide.

20
00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:03.067
And keep state shared and locked, or teammates corrupt each other's view of reality.

21
00:01:04.267 --> 00:01:05.733
Here is a small example.

22
00:01:06.033 --> 00:01:09.167
The first block declares an S3 bucket for raw logs.

23
00:01:09.467 --> 00:01:15.133
The second turns on versioning, and refers to the bucket's id, so Terraform knows the order.

24
00:01:15.433 --> 00:01:19.933
Running plan reports two to add, zero to change, zero to destroy.

25
00:01:21.133 --> 00:01:22.667
Here is the real payoff.

26
00:01:22.967 --> 00:01:29.467
The whole AWS footprint lives in a Terraform repo: the cluster, buckets, roles and registry.

27
00:01:29.767 --> 00:01:36.200
When a reviewer questions a new permission, the plan in the pull request shows exactly one policy line changing.

28
00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:41.194
And a staging environment is the same code with a different variables file.

29
00:01:41.194 --> 00:01:45.200
An identical stack in twenty minutes, not a week of archaeology.

30
00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:47.225
Let's recap.

31
00:01:47.225 --> 00:01:50.733
Terraform is infrastructure as code, declaratively.

32
00:01:51.033 --> 00:01:54.100
Plan is a reviewable diff, and apply executes it.

33
00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:58.800
Minus slash plus means destroy and recreate, so read plans carefully.

34
00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:02.067
And protect your state with remote storage and locking.
